From: Marco Müller <hello@annoyedmilk.ch>
Subject: [PATCH] riscv: esp32s31: sync the caches for new code

The I-cache of each ESP32-S31 core refills from PSRAM, not from the
shared D-cache.  With write-back PSRAM, new code can stay in the
D-cache.  fence.i also does not invalidate the I-cache: a write, a
flush and a call of the same address gave the old code in 292 of 300
runs, until a timer tick replaced the line.

Ask the firmware to write back the D-cache and to invalidate the
I-cache.

Signed-off-by: Marco Müller <hello@annoyedmilk.ch>
---
diff --git a/arch/riscv/include/asm/cacheflush.h b/arch/riscv/include/asm/cacheflush.h
--- a/arch/riscv/include/asm/cacheflush.h
+++ b/arch/riscv/include/asm/cacheflush.h
@@ -8,8 +8,19 @@
 
 #include <linux/mm.h>
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_ESP32S31_CACHE
+void esp32s31_icache_sync(void);
+#endif
+
 static inline void local_flush_icache_all(void)
 {
+#ifdef CONFIG_ESP32S31_CACHE
+	/*
+	 * The ESP32-S31 I-cache refills from PSRAM, not from the shared
+	 * D-cache, and fence.i does not invalidate it.
+	 */
+	esp32s31_icache_sync();
+#endif
 	asm volatile ("fence.i" ::: "memory");
 }
 
